`#realtime` — Parsing and Round-Trip
=====================================

`#realtime` is an orthogonal effect annotation marking a function as
safe to call from a real-time context (audio render callback, kernel
interrupt handler, signal handler). The full enforcement rules (no
heap allocation, no blocking, no unbounded work, only realtime-clean
callees) live in a separate analyzer pass; this file pins the *runtime
neutrality* of the annotation:

- A `#realtime` function compiles and runs identically to an
  unmarked function with the same body.
- `#realtime` may combine with `#pure` and with `#reads`/`#writes`
  (orthogonal axis).
- `#realtime` parses on free functions, interface methods, and
  function types.

File-local prefix `Rt`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


### `#realtime` zero-arg function

A `#realtime` function with no body-side effects runs as normal.

    #realtime
    rt_answer() -> int = 42

    #test
    test_rt_returns_constant() -> unit
        assert(rt_answer() == 42, "rt_answer should return 42")


### `#realtime` combined with `#pure`

The two annotations are orthogonal — `#pure` (no module-level effects,
proof-friendly) and `#realtime` (no heap/blocking) describe different
disciplines. A function may carry both.

    #pure
    #realtime
    rt_square(x: int) -> int = x * x

    #test
    test_rt_pure_combo() -> unit
        assert(rt_square(7) == 49, "7² = 49")
        assert(rt_square(-3) == 9, "(-3)² = 9")


### `#realtime` on a function type

The `#realtime` suffix on a function type participates in the same
mechanism as `#pure` — at indirect call sites, the slot's effect
signature is matched against the actual.

    rt_apply(f: (int)->int #realtime, x: int) -> int = f(x)

    #realtime
    rt_double(x: int) -> int = x + x

    #test
    test_rt_func_type_indirect() -> unit
        assert(rt_apply(rt_double, 21) == 42, "rt_apply(rt_double, 21)")
